    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back to when you were a kid or in school, did you have trouble sitting still back then as well?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah, actually. Even back in grade school, I was always squirming around. I remember my teachers—Mrs. Gable, I think her name was, third grade—she used to tell my parents I was a bright kid but just completely scattered. I was always losing my pencils, calling out answers before she even finished asking the question, that sort of thing. She'd constantly have to remind me to finish my worksheets before I could run out for recess. \n\nRecess was the best part of the day, honestly. We used to play kickball out on this big dirt lot behind the school. It wasn't paved or anything, just pure dust. You'd come home with your sneakers completely orange. My mom hated it. She was always scrubbing those canvas sneakers in the sink with a toothbrush and some kind of strong lye soap. She swore by that soap for everything—getting out grass stains, grease, you name it. My dad was a mechanic, so he always came home covered in grease. He worked at the old Ford dealership downtown before they tore it down to build that strip mall. \n\nAnyway, what was I saying? Oh, right, school. Yeah, I always had trouble sitting at a desk. I just needed to be doing something with my hands, you know? That's probably why I ended up in construction.",
